<section class="block">
<h2>What was reported</h2>
<p>Microsoft's results for the quarter to 30 September showed Azure revenue growing 48%, ahead of analyst estimates, with guidance for faster growth in the current quarter<sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-1">1</a></sup>. Capital spending of $45.2 billion came in below the company's own projection, which it attributed to delivery timing rather than a change of plan<sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-1">1</a></sup>, and its finance chief said demand still exceeds supply<sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-2">2</a></sup>. Analysts also noted capital expenditure guidance was held steady<sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-3">3</a></sup>.</p>
</section>

<section class="block">
<h2>Why this matters to engineering leaders</h2>
<p>When the largest providers are still building as fast as they can and demand outruns them, capacity for AI workloads, especially accelerators in specific regions, is not guaranteed on demand. That affects launch dates, data-residency plans and cost. The response is the same discipline used for any scarce resource: know what you need, decide what matters most, reserve for that, and have a fallback.</p>
<figure class="dgm">
<div class="dgm-stack">
<div class="dgm-tier"><small>Critical</small><div class="dgm-nodes"><span class="hl">Customer-facing inference<em>reserved capacity, home region</em></span></div></div>
<div class="dgm-tier"><small>Important</small><div class="dgm-nodes"><span>Internal assistants<em>on demand, can queue</em></span><span>Evaluation runs<em>scheduled off-peak</em></span></div></div>
<div class="dgm-tier"><small>Flexible</small><div class="dgm-nodes"><span>Batch enrichment<em>spot or preemptible</em></span><span>Training and fine-tuning<em>any region allowed by data rules</em></span></div></div>
<div class="dgm-tier"><small>Fallback</small><div class="dgm-nodes"><span class="hl">Second provider or model<em>tested, not theoretical</em></span><span>Smaller private model<em>floor for critical paths</em></span></div></div>
</div>
<figcaption>Tier workloads by how much a shortage would hurt, and match each tier to a capacity strategy.</figcaption>
</figure>
</section>

<section class="block">
<h2>Let the scheduler enforce your priorities</h2>
<p>On Kubernetes, the scheduler decides where each workload runs, and pod priority and preemption decide what happens when resources run out: higher-priority pods are scheduled first, and lower-priority ones can be evicted to make room<sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-4">4</a></sup><sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-5">5</a></sup>. Giving customer-facing inference a higher priority class than batch jobs means a capacity squeeze lands on work that can wait.</p>
<figure class="fig"><div class="im"><img src="/assets/img/ai-capacity-crunch-cloud-planning/scheduling.webp" alt="Diagram of Kubernetes scheduling: a client creates new pods, the API server stores them in etcd, the kube-scheduler assigns pods without a node to a node, and kubelets on each node launch them" width="1200" height="935" loading="lazy"></div><figcaption>How the scheduler places new workloads; priority classes decide who goes first, and who is evicted, when capacity is short. <span class="credit">Source: <a href="https://kubernetes.io/blog/2023/01/12/protect-mission-critical-pods-priorityclass/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Kubernetes blog, Sunny Bhambhani</a> · CC BY 4.0</span></figcaption></figure>
<div class="callout"><p>In a shortage, something will wait. Decide in advance what it is, and encode that decision in the platform rather than in a meeting during the incident.</p></div>
</section>

<section class="block">
<h2>A capacity plan in four steps</h2>
<figure class="dgm">
<ol class="dgm-flow g3">
<li><b>Measure</b><span>Accelerator hours, tokens and latency per workload and region</span></li>
<li><b>Tier</b><span>Critical, important and flexible, agreed with the business</span></li>
<li class="hl"><b>Reserve</b><span>Committed capacity for critical work where data rules require it</span></li>
<li><b>Prioritise</b><span>Priority classes and quotas so shortages hit flexible work first</span></li>
<li class="hl"><b>Hedge</b><span>A second provider, region or model tested with real traffic</span></li>
<li><b>Review</b><span>Monthly: usage against reservations, cost, and upcoming launches</span></li>
</ol>
<figcaption>Capacity planning for AI is FinOps plus resilience.</figcaption>
</figure>
<table class="tbl">
<thead><tr><th>Lever</th><th>Helps with</th><th>Watch out for</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td>Reservations and commitments</td><td>Guaranteed capacity for critical work</td><td>Paying for idle capacity if forecasts are wrong</td></tr>
<tr><td>Priority classes and quotas</td><td>Shortages fall on flexible work</td><td>Needs agreed tiers, not engineering guesses</td></tr>
<tr><td>Second provider or region</td><td>Availability and negotiating position</td><td>Data residency and quality differences</td></tr>
<tr><td>Smaller or routed models</td><td>Less capacity needed per task</td><td>Quality must be proven on your evaluation set</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
</section>

<section class="block">
<h2>CTO take</h2>
<p>Treat AI capacity as a supply chain. Know your demand by workload, reserve for what is critical in the regions your data rules require, and make sure a shortage degrades flexible work first. Regional constraints differ: residency rules in the EU, India, Saudi Arabia and elsewhere can limit where you are allowed to fail over, so check them before you count a region as a fallback (<a href="/regions/">by region</a>). Related reading: <a href="/insights/focus-finops-ai-cost-allocation/">AI cost allocation with FOCUS</a>, <a href="/insights/kubernetes-dra-gpu-scheduling/">GPU scheduling with DRA</a>, <a href="/insights/llm-model-routing/">LLM model routing</a>, <a href="/case-studies/ai-provider-resilience-cps-230-insurer/">AI provider resilience under CPS 230</a> and the <a href="/insights/tech-news-2026-10-29-2/">tech news update for 29 October</a>.</p>
</section>

